{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact bd6fb3831867a279539fd03d464ea482cb6e30eb and all nineteen artifacts, primary63390f6b first, under review29485844/promiseae8d199e and governing requestadd29762. The clock and request-lifecycle defects from verdict68d3a03a are fixed at this head. The remaining already-open-browser/profile condition is not met.\n\nAt ui/src/lib/store.ts:31, useWorkroom retains the prior Status through every pending wait. Its probe supplies only running/verified/total; ui/src/lib/rebuild.ts:16 adds a generic qualifier, and App.tsx:47 continues exposing the retained projection. Neither that wire nor the Status retention path establishes that the old projection remains interpretable under the resident's current fold profile. Requester disposition6cf3efa3 explicitly preserves the original condition that a profile-invalidated projection remains unavailable.\n\nThere is also a concrete checkpoint-backed case in which even that qualifier disappears. In an isolated actual Workspace test, start from a verified snapshot, change the selected projection-profile identity, and pause the replacement interpretation at the existing pre-publication gate. Snapshot correctly remains pending, but RebuildProgress returns Started:false/running:false because this path reused the signed kernel checkpoint and did not start a cold audit. handleRebuild forwards that response unchanged (server.go:538-559; app.go:2369). The real useWorkroom, driven with this observed response shape, retains exactly the old Status and exposes no rebuild qualifier. With running:true it retains the same old projection and only adds the generic notice. Evidence6d4c1e50 contains both the actual Workspace probe and controlled-endpoint real-hook failures. These are complementary isolated tests, not a claim of a deployed browser/service upgrade experiment; the hook's test-only word current is a sentinel for an absent qualifier, not a literal production label.\n\nCorrect the existing parent outcome so an already-open browser can distinguish a demonstrably compatible retained projection from one invalidated by profile change, including checkpoint-backed interpretation with no cold-audit progress. An unverifiable compatibility state cannot silently keep the old projection available. Preserve useful qualification for the explicitly permitted same-profile case and the verified-current boundary for all authoritative reads and acts. Test both the Workspace/HTTP producer and actual browser consumer, including the period before replacement publication, and retain an omission control that fails if profile safety is removed. Do not treat a rebuild progress boolean as proof of interpretation compatibility. If the correction changes a layer's response contract, update docs/reference/architecture.md in that same exact head and publish its candidate artifact.\n\nThe assignment gap for first-time-reader case C is now resolved by requester's effective disposition6cf3efa3 and assigned child20301/e855a8ca. The child owes adoption and full implementation if adopted, or explicit ratifier decline and final guidance; a design-only landing cannot close it. This is a scope/acceptance disposition, not adoption of proposal20195 or proof of case C delivery. Preserve that parent/child mapping in the final reporting artifact. Case C's pending decision need not block a corrected parent. No new endpoint or trust policy is authorized by this review.\n\nArchitecture: layers3,5 and7 are affected. Shared notification, kernel verification, cancellation-independent snapshot flight and atomic publication remain correctly separated. The current head changes no server wire contract, but browser retention fails to respect the interpretation boundary described above. All four documentation artifacts reach both the current waiter and clock producer artifacts. Security: the named exposure is an unresolved interpretation/trust-boundary defect, even though publishing an act still rechecks current authority. No key, secret or signature change is present. This is assigned implementation, not an authority-bearing decision shortcut. Simplification: the corrected transitive retirement barrier and owned AbortController lifecycle address the previous defects; use one coherent interpretation-validity rule and avoid treating UI labels or an additional cache as verification.\n\nValidation: the whole uncached Go1.26.7 suite passes, including cmd/gs456.212s, MCP152.190s, service84.861s, docset104.742s and wireparity2.347s; whole-Go vet passes. All214 UI tests and make ui-check pass, with a clean tracked checkout and reproducible embedded output. All19 previews are ready/byte-exact, all4 documentation chains reach the current clock/waiter, and candidates were live with ordinary inherited staleness and no describes_superseded_world flag when checked. Retained two/three-lifetime clock probes pass three runs; all three retained real-hook lifecycle probes pass. Six actual omissions are caught: transitive barrier, probe abort, API signal forwarding, within-wait bound, late-answer guard and release after failure. Full optional performance was not rerun: unchanged harness/measurement limits remain as previously reviewed, with no depth20k or physical-resource claim. Main dfbf4089 is pushed and CI34077923649/Security34077923676 pass, closing the separate wireparity prerequisite.\n\nCorrect on the existing implementation commitment, cite this finding and requester disposition in fresh exact-head artifacts, then request independent review. Sealed exact-path succession, source/workroom/receipt pushes, CI and owned-worktree cleanup still govern landing.\n","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:63390f6b081cb2b5d59e02b1632a46600dd64626","binding":"assigned","head":"bd6fb3831867a279539fd03d464ea482cb6e30eb","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ae8d199e58ef07ced4ae1881d3fc0002bf91a994\",\"git:s